--- !u!114 &1582174451
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1582174449}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Solar System is a collection of the Sun and all the objects that are
    gravitationally bound to it. The Sun is at the centre and contains most of the
    Solar System\u2019s mass. Its gravity provides the force that keeps the planets
    moving in orbit.\n\nThere are eight planets, arranged from the Sun as Mercury,
    Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. Each planet travels
    around the Sun along an orbit. These orbits are not perfect circles; they are
    slightly elliptical.\n\nThe four inner planets\u2014Mercury, Venus, Earth, and
    Mars\u2014are called terrestrial planets because they have solid, rocky surfaces.
    Beyond Mars are the outer planets. Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants, while Uranus
    and Neptune are commonly classified as ice giants.\n\nThe distance of a planet
    from the Sun strongly affects its orbital period. Planets closer to the Sun generally
    complete their orbits more quickly, while planets farther away take much longer.
    Mercury completes one revolution in about 88 Earth days, whereas Neptune takes
    about 165 Earth years.\n\nThe orbital paths can be visualized in a 3D simulation.
    Highlighting one orbit at a time helps students understand the order and relative
    arrangement of the planets. Showing all the orbits together provides a clear
    picture of the Solar System\u2019s structure.\n\nThe planets also rotate on their
    own axes while revolving around the Sun. Rotation and revolution are different
    motions: rotation is spinning around an axis, while revolution is movement around
    another object.\n\nIn the 3D experience, the student can observe the Sun at the
    centre, select each planet, highlight its orbital path, and compare the different
    orbital periods."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
